From 10e6401b937b3fd28801ccd19be31d36b5e3096c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vair <79313360+nicolasvair@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 14:41:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(record): recording through a loop - a monotone recording
 clock and a journal of the passes (objekat 0050)

Behind EngineBehaviour::recordsThroughLoop() (false in the stock engine, true in objekat unless OBJ_REC_STOCK is set).

The recorder is paired with the play head's UNLOOPED clock, which every setLoopRange/setPosition rebases, so a loop
edit under a recording, or a punch-in during a play that already loops (N laps further), breaks the file's position
on the timeline. Under the flag:

- EditPlaybackContext: a recording clock, anchored by the audio thread at the first playing block after the arm to the
  head's LOOPED position, then one sample per sample of the reference stream and never rebased. It replaces the
  unlooped time in getUnloopedEditTimeOfNextBlock, getUnloopedPosition and globalStreamTimeToEditTimeUnlooped (armed but
  not anchored yet: the looped position of the block, the inputs being handed over before process() anchors).
  A journal of the passes (clock sample, timeline sample) at every discontinuity of the head, written in
  NodePlaybackContext::process from referenceSampleRangeToSplitTimelineRange into a lock-free buffer drained by the
  transport's tick; getRecordJournal() for the host. Diagnostic: how often the head's own clock moved against ours.
- PlayHead: setLoopRangeRollingIn (loop bounds change without losing the head's place).
- TransportControl: no 2 s minimum and a start from the caret; a change of `looping` or of the range is applied at
  once (applyLoopToPlayHeadNow) instead of stopping the recording; the rec end is read on the recording clock.
- WaveInputDevice: isLooping=false at the stop (no split into _take_N, one continuous file); the file is never deleted
  because the end time looks earlier; a block is written whatever time the clock says once the take has started.
- MidiInputDevice: isLooping=false at the stop; the stamps of one capture are monotone.
- getDefaultRecordingParameters: the take starts at the press, not at the loop's start.

Proven by tools/scenario_record_loop_proof.py: stock = a 1.5 s punch-in on a looping play gives a 2.000 s file that is
entirely silent, and a loop switched on under a recording ends it; with this patch no silence, the recording goes on,
and the journal matches the render to the sample on every pass. scenario_record (F, F2, R) and scenario_midi_record
unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
